# Completion review: auto-band first-slice implementation checkpoint

Date: 2026-10-03
Scope: first `add-auto-band-seam` implementation slice and its current validation
state. Reserve scope decisions, controller/integration, calibration, production
deployment, and OpenSpec archiving remain outstanding.

## Decisions retained

- Band channels default to `thrifty` only while `OMNIROUTE_AUTO_BANDS` is on.
  With the flag off, omitted tier stays unset for upstream default handling;
  explicit tiers retain upstream behavior.
- Unrated models pass only `low`; they receive no synthetic fitness score and
  remain outside mid/high calibration populations.
- The old `agt` role maps to `general + tools`.
- Enabled band `thrifty` channels order eligible rungs `free → keyless →
subscription → cheap → premium`. Plain upstream `auto:thrifty` ordering is
  unchanged. The flag-off path bypasses band order, quality/capability filters,
  reserve, and account narrowing.
- Reserve removes reserved account IDs before band rung ordering and dispatch;
  it retains candidates with at least one account left and drops candidates
  whose allowed IDs are all reserved.
- Explicit `:free` keeps upstream provider/model classification, including
  eligible keyless OpenCode models. It is not a per-connection zero-spend
  guarantee.

## First-slice implementation

Band grammar, flag-controlled parser/filter seams, band-only `thrifty`
ordering, exact account-rung metadata, disabled-path pass-through hooks, and the
quality/capability filter hook are implemented. Independent review found no
remaining quality-hook findings. Reserve configuration is implemented, but
capacity policy remains unfinalized pending source-contract tracing: the
initial 1B/500M example came from a UI test, and mixed monthly limits in a
production pool are unconfirmed. The reserve writer is tracing
`shared_pool.tokensPerMonth` versus per-model `monthlyTokens` before the user
chooses scope. The user resolved disabled-reserve behavior to a full stop for
calculations, refresh, and diagnostics; its spec update and regression test are
pending. The reserve controller and quality/reserve integration remain
incomplete.

The initial implementation report records 23 band tests and 284 upstream
Auto-Combo tests passing, plus Prettier and diff checks passing. The fork release
and deployment guide now records the upstream rebase/tag procedure; its
documentation link check passed for 168 documents and 1174 links. The confirmed
selection defect is fixed in the final `resolveAutoStrategyOrder` path: the
router strategy now runs within each billing rung, and exact mapped accounts
retain rung order through score fallback. The regression covers a premium LKGP
pin, a higher-scoring premium model, LKGP within a rung, a stale direct account
pin against an allowlist, an unresolved null-connection target, and flag-off
upstream behavior. A further regression covers a quota-cutoff-blocked free
account beside a routable premium account; blocked accounts stay as terminal
fallbacks after every routable rung. The focused routing suite passes 7/7, and
27 direct resolver, stream, and quota tests pass. The independent reviewer
closed both routing findings.

## Validation and environment limits

- Before the routing repair, fresh validation passed typecheck with zero
  diagnostics, ESLint on 8 files with zero errors, and 31 regression files / 284
  tests. Scoped results: routing 7/7, direct upstream resolver/stream/quota
  27/27, quality E2E 4/4, config 13/13, capabilities 8/8, filter 8/8, and five
  focused band suites 37/37 before the reserve-config extension. The quality
  owner reported 201/201 across 28 files before WIP reserve tests. A later
  partial run passed 208 tests but could not import the in-progress reserve
  module. Reserve files/tests are excluded from the first commit, and these
  counts do not certify reserve. ESLint and Prettier pass on the changed routing
  source and regression test; the last reported OpenSSE typecheck had zero
  diagnostics.
- Independent reviews cleared the quality hook and reserve configuration.
  Reserve scope is not final: the policy for accounts in one pool with different
  monthly limits remains undecided. Disabled-reserve full-stop behavior is
  approved but needs a spec update and regression test. Controller/integration
  and calibration remain incomplete.
- A separate configuration recovery finding is fixed and independently
  verified: 7/7 focused tests cover same-mtime `EACCES` recovery and malformed
  range warning deduplication; typecheck, lint, and Prettier passed for that
  fix.
- The isolated-copy rehearsal passed its three-row migration-ledger rekey;
  missing upstream migrations were replayed, and the second init reported 0
  pending; FMO data/schema stayed unchanged; trigger
  savepoint rollback checks and SQLite `quick_check` passed. It used a fresh
  read-only snapshot in a task-owned temporary directory and did not modify or
  restart production. The procedure and results are documented in
  [docs/ops/AUTO_BANDS_MIGRATION_REHEARSAL.md](docs/ops/AUTO_BANDS_MIGRATION_REHEARSAL.md).
- The unmodified optimized Next build remains incomplete. An earlier local
  attempt stopped near 1.4 GB free disk. Later preflight began at 19 GiB free
  with a 14 GiB guard and ended at 19,480,548 KiB after both initialization
  passes. The `--rm` container metadata is gone and the exact-container Docker
  journal query returned no records, so the precise build exit cause cannot be
  recovered. Do not retry or take production action from this checkpoint;
  OpenSpec task 1.4 remains unchecked.
- The observed migration overlap is fork IDs 164–166 (`fmo_pools`,
  `fmo_pool_decisions`, `fmo_pool_live_seam`) versus upstream migration names
  `retire_microsoft_designer_web`, `retire_felo_web`, and
  `retire_gpl_derived_providers`. The runner also reconciled a rename from 163
  to 169 and warned that version-only tracking can skip and rerun migrations.
  The isolated-copy repair/rehearsal results are recorded above. Production
  changes remain unauthorized, and permanent migration mapping/compatibility
  code is forbidden.

## Remaining scope

- Complete remaining reserve controller/integration, then validate the enabled
  reserve in shadow mode and verify account-level routing. Preserve the verified
  invariants: exact provider/model/account mappings, authoritative allowlists,
  blocked quota accounts after all routable rungs, and unchanged ordinary
  upstream behavior.
- Trace the authoritative monthly-limit contract (`shared_pool.tokensPerMonth`
  versus per-model `monthlyTokens`) and ask the user to confirm scope; production
  pools with mixed limits are not verified. Update the spec and add a regression
  for the approved disabled-reserve full stop across calculations, refresh, and
  diagnostics. Keep the pool-scope decision in TODO; do not add unapproved
  alternatives to the spec.
- Complete OpenSpec tasks 1.4 and 4.3: baseline build/start and live full-ID
  logging. Task 4.5 is complete in
  `docs/ops/FORK_RELEASE_AND_DEPLOYMENT.md`; its documentation link check passed
  for 168 documents and 1174 links. Extend the guide with calibration, reserve
  rollout, and rollback before production rollout.
- Calibration remains blocked until the user chooses the percentile method for
  the 33rd/67th cut points. That decision must precede the calibration report.
- Correct the declared `QuotaSnapshotRow` contract in a separate upstream
  contract slice. The live getter returns camelCase fields, and `isExhausted`
  remains numeric `0 | 1`; the exported type still declares snake_case fields.
  Reserve currently uses a local strict camelCase boundary without a
  dual-shape shim.
- `add-systemone-decisions-route` was approved and frozen on 2026-10-03 with 23
  tasks, 9 requirements, and 27 scenarios; OpenSpec validation passed. Its
  separately owned runtime implementation has started.
- Reconcile migration history and refresh the deployment source reference
  before deployment.
- Deploy and validate before archiving the OpenSpec packages.

This checkpoint is ready for the authorized feature-branch commit; production
deployment remains unauthorized.
